WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Today, we're going to be speaking with Jonathan&nbsp;
Cohn, who's a senior national correspondent at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:11.520
Huff Post and also author of the book The 10&nbsp;
Year War, Obamacare and the Unfinished Crusade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.320
for Universal Coverage. Great to have you on.
Jonathan, thanks for having me on the show.&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.560
So to start with you for a while, the way&nbsp;
that particularly the right but even sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.320
Democrats would talk about Obamacare was this sort&nbsp;
of like appendage on to what was there before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.240
which could be cut off and separated kind of&nbsp;
at any time. The language of what we can we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:40.640
repeal it and we'll put something else in place.&nbsp;
Correct me if I'm wrong, but we seem to be getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.400
beyond that to where Obamacare is no
longer this&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.280
kind of standalone appendage to the health&nbsp;
care system. And it's just more part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:57.360
status quo. And it's it's becoming more of the&nbsp;
system that we would have to modify or work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.400
off of if we want to do further changes. Is&nbsp;
my perception accurate as far as that goes?&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.280
Yeah, I mean, I think just by default, almost&nbsp;
when you have something that exist for as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:18.160
as the Affordable Care Act has existed, when&nbsp;
you when it's so entrenched in our system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:26.640
now that to pull it out would be very hard. And&nbsp;
it's people have insurance that they rely upon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:35.360
The health care industry is now sort of reoriented&nbsp;
itself around having this program in place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.320
And so pulling it out now is you have&nbsp;
to pull out a whole bunch of wiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.240
that goes with it. And it's interesting&nbsp;
because historically, this is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.760
obstacles to passing reform, right. Is that&nbsp;
the status quo is always hard to change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.480
Well, now the Affordable Care Act, that's why it&nbsp;
was hard to pass the Affordable Care Act. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.000
why it was such a fight and continued to be such a&nbsp;
fight. But now it's been around for 10 years. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.920
know, it's now it's part of the status quo and&nbsp;
it's hard to change. And I think you see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.800
in part by the behavior of the Republicans&nbsp;
who don't really talk about repeal anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.120
which, as you know, it wasn't that long ago.&nbsp;
It was it was all they could talk about.&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:20.080
Yeah. And sometimes I hear the criticism from&nbsp;
folks who believe that the Affordable Care Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.560
doesn't go nearly far enough. And I'm under that&nbsp;
umbrella to some degree. But but not you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.120
there's certainly a spectrum within that.&nbsp;
Sometimes you hear the critique that Barack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.200
Obama should have started at the position&nbsp;
of Medicare for all and then negotiated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.000
something that would have been better than what we&nbsp;
got. I don't subscribe to that exactly, because, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.960
mean, he wasn't for Medicare for all. It would be&nbsp;
unlikely to expect someone to start a negotiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.160
with a position that's not even their stated&nbsp;
position. But I do think that maybe there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.840
would have been a way to to end up with a&nbsp;
public option, having chronicled the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.840
this all came together. Is it unreasonable&nbsp;
for me to think that some other approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.760
might have given us a public option back in 2010?
So I wouldn't say unreasonable, certainly. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.840
you can any critique is reasonable, and I think&nbsp;
reasonable people can debate about this. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.960
say I'm very skeptical of that argument for two&nbsp;
reasons. So No. One, in studying the history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:28.000
health care, know there was a century of working&nbsp;
on this, basically, and almost without exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.440
it was a failure. Reforming health care is really&nbsp;
hard, reforming it. And given the constraints of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.880
the United States government, where the Senate and&nbsp;
the power of special interests is even harder for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.280
the country politically was in a different place&nbsp;
10 years ago than it is today. I mean, I think a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.440
lot of people who are more progressive minded&nbsp;
don't remember that the country the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.120
conversation was just a more conservative&nbsp;
conversation. We were still in the back end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.920
the Reagan era where everyone was very skeptical&nbsp;
of government and the only reason health care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.280
reform got as far as it did, and I talk about this&nbsp;
a lot in the book, is that there was a real effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.400
for the 10 years leading up to the Affordable&nbsp;
Care Act. So the 10 years before the ten year war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.200
by Democrats to really get together and say, look,&nbsp;
the only way we're going to get through this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.800
the only way we're going to overcome our past&nbsp;
failures is to all get on the same page. And and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.880
Medicare for all was just not in the conversation.&nbsp;
It's not like it was a day where had been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.720
subject of a presidential campaign for two&nbsp;
consecutive elections where you had some visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.880
proponents who had a big grassroots movement&nbsp;
behind it. So, you know, if Obama had gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.960
in and sort of offering to do Medicare for all at&nbsp;
the beginning, I think just there would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.440
the whole thing would have fallen apart. And in&nbsp;
terms of getting a public option through now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.600
obviously he did endorse a public option. He&nbsp;
ran on a public option. It was part of his plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.640
At the end of the day, they took it out and a&nbsp;
lot of progressives felt like he never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.200
fought for it. And, you know, there is something,&nbsp;
you know, there's maybe something to that. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.800
a lot of progressives felt like he could have&nbsp;
pushed harder. The flip side of the argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.680
is the votes were not there in the United States&nbsp;
Senate. There were a whole group of senators. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.360
also in the House, there were some there are some&nbsp;
conservative Democrats, although it was less of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.120
issue there. But in the Senate, you had a bunch of&nbsp;
conservative Democrats who would not vote for it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:21.840
and they had no margin for error. They needed&nbsp;
every single vote. And there I don't it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.960
hard to imagine a scenario where you could&nbsp;
get it through, especially given how opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.120
to sort of the sort of most conservative members&nbsp;
of the caucus. I mean, when they got to the bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.440
and they were getting to the end, they got to&nbsp;
fifty five, fifty six votes. They couldn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.240
Joe Lieberman. They couldn't get Ben Nelson.&nbsp;
Both Lieberman and Nelson were dead set against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.160
the public option. And there were others&nbsp;
who were not so vocal about it but were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:49.040
also against it. So I have a hard time seeing&nbsp;
how a bill passes with a public option in it.&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.920
Yeah, I mean that to a lesser extent. We saw this&nbsp;
with the 15 dollar minimum wage in the of it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

